  "slug": "optical-fidelity-dimensional-3797",
  "summary": "Recent preprints report six-dimensional optical storage, snapshot infrared sensing and 250 kHz wavefront shaping through multimode fibers.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is the use of multiple optical degrees of freedom, spanning polarization, spectral and spatial information, to encode or control light beyond simple intensity. A six-dimensional storage scheme uses polarized light in laser-induced nanostructures and reports 99.45% raw accuracy. Gradient metasurface pixels map spectral and polarization content into spatial patterns for snapshot infrared optical sensing. A phase-only MEMS modulator with 8,192 elements and 250 kHz refresh performs wavefront shaping through multimode fiber, reporting 70% light delivery. The applications differ, so the dominant thread is multidimensional readout and control rather than a single shared method. The new element across these preprints is the pairing of pixelated polarization-spectral encoding with fast, high-element-count phase modulation.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-26"
}
